<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">On the Hodge and Tate conjectures for moduli spaces of curves</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sam Payne
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We survey recent progress on the cohomology of moduli spaces of stable curves through the lens of the Hodge and Tate conjectures, especially their generalized coniveau forms, which relate Hodge structures and <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic Galois representations on cohomology to algebraic cycles. We explain how the inductive structure of the boundary stratification verifies these conjectures in a surprisingly wide range of cases, describe the guiding inspiration from arithmetic, and discuss open problems and directions for future research.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Hodge conjecture and Tate conjecture are two central threads in a web of interrelated conjectures relating cohomology theories for algebraic varieties to the properties of algebraic cycles and categories of motives. These conjectures are wide open in general, and known in only a few special cases. Progress has typically involved the study of varieties that move in nontrivial families, such as abelian varieties and K3 surfaces, using degeneration arguments and the properties of special varieties with extra endomorphisms, such as CM abelian varieties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent progress in understanding the cohomology of moduli spaces of stable curves opens a new avenue for the investigation of these conjectures. These moduli spaces do not deform in families, but instead constitute an infinite discrete collection of spaces whose cohomology groups contain rich and complicated Hodge structures and Galois representations. The geometric role usually played by variation in families is here played by the tautological gluing and forgetful morphisms, together with the boundary stratification and weight arguments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The recent cohomology computations are discussed in more detail in Larson’s survey article <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lar26</a>]</cite>. The purpose of this article is to explain their consequences for the Hodge and Tate conjectures, especially in their generalized coniveau forms.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Main statements</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout, <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the smooth and proper Deligne–Mumford moduli stack of stable <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-pointed genus <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curves, and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\subset\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,n}\subset\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the open substack of smooth curves. Except where explicitly stated otherwise, cohomology is rational singular cohomology over <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-adic cohomology after base change to a separable closure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The inductive structure of the stratification by topological types of stable curves, combined with topological properties of the locally closed strata and some basic mixed Hodge theory, allow for the verification of the generalized Hodge and Tate conjectures in a surprisingly wide range of cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="ThmTheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="ThmTheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A</span></span><span id="ThmTheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="ThmTheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="ThmTheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="ThmTheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Hodge conjecture holds on <math id="ThmTheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> for:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">algebraic cycles of codimension 1 and 2, for all </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">algebraic cycles of codimension 3, for </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\not\in\{7,8,9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\not\in\{7,8,9\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and for all </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">algebraic cycles of dimension less than or equal to </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for all </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="ThmTheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="ThmTheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Tate Conjecture holds on <math id="ThmTheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, over any finitely generated field, in the same range of cases.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The theorem is proved by showing that the relevant cohomology groups are generated by tautological classes. Throughout, we assume that <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> are nonnegative integers such that <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, so the moduli space of stable curves <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The Hodge conjecture and Tate conjecture pertain to the subspaces of even cohomology groups spanned by algebraic cycle classes. They have generalizations which apply to subspaces of cohomology groups, in both even and odd degrees, which are supported on an algebraic subset of specified codimension. The classical Hodge and Tate conjectures are the respective special cases where the cohomological degree is twice the codimension of the algebraic subset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#ThmTheorem1" title="Theorem A. ‣ 1.1. Main statements ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Hodge and Tate conjectures for moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> concerns the image of the cycle class map in even degree. Theorems <a href="#ThmTheorem2" title="Theorem B. ‣ 1.1. Main statements ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Hodge and Tate conjectures for moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> and <a href="#ThmTheorem3" title="Theorem C. ‣ 1.1. Main statements ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Hodge and Tate conjectures for moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> concern substructures rather than individual cohomology classes; they address when Hodge and Galois substructures are supported on algebraic subsets of the predicted codimension. See the conjecture statements in Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Hodge and Tate conjectures ‣ On the Hodge and Tate conjectures for moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Fix <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d:=3g-3+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d:=3g-3+n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="ThmTheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="ThmTheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem B</span></span><span id="ThmTheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="ThmTheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="ThmTheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="ThmTheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The generalized Hodge conjecture holds for <math id="ThmTheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, as follows:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for even </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for all </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=6</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\not\in\{7,8,9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\not\in\{7,8,9\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and for all </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for odd </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 13</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for all </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for </span><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2d-j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2d-j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for </span><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq j\leq 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq j\leq 15</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and for all </span><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned above, the even degree cases are proved by showing that these cohomology groups are generated by tautological classes. The arguments for odd degrees are similar in spirit; see the examples in Section <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4. Odd examples ‣ 2. Hodge and Tate conjectures ‣ On the Hodge and Tate conjectures for moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="ThmTheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="ThmTheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem C</span></span><span id="ThmTheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="ThmTheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="ThmTheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="ThmTheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="ThmTheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a finitely generated field. We assume <math id="ThmTheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> has either characteristic zero, or positive characteristic <math id="ThmTheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\neq\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\neq\ell</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="ThmTheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is ordinary for <math id="ThmTheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the generalized Tate conjecture holds for <math id="ThmTheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}((\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})_{L^{s}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}((\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})_{L^{s}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="ThmTheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="ThmTheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="ThmTheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> as above (Theorem <a href="#ThmTheorem2" title="Theorem B. ‣ 1.1. Main statements ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Hodge and Tate conjectures for moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, cases (1)–(4)).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In all of the cases covered by Theorems <a href="#ThmTheorem2" title="Theorem B. ‣ 1.1. Main statements ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Hodge and Tate conjectures for moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>-<a href="#ThmTheorem3" title="Theorem C. ‣ 1.1. Main statements ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Hodge and Tate conjectures for moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> for odd degrees <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the cohomology group <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, with its rational Hodge structure or Galois action after tensoring with <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, is a direct sum of Tate twists of <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}:=W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{M}_{1,11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}:=W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{M}_{1,11})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{16}:=W_{15}H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{1,15})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{16}:=W_{15}H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{1,15})</annotation></semantics></math>. The ordinarity condition means we require <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is ordinary for the motivic structures <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever their Tate twists appear.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For non-ordinary primes, the generalized Tate conjecture predicts the existence of a higher codimension algebraic subset that supports the given structure. For instance, there should be a codimension 3 algebraic subset that supports <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{11}((\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{2}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{11}((\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{1,11})_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_{2}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>. See Problem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem4" title="Problem 6.4. ‣ 6. Future outlook ‣ On the Hodge and Tate conjectures for moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>.
This ordinarity condition can be made explicit in all of the cases under consideration.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For even <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the motivic structures are of Tate type and all primes are ordinary. For odd <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 13</annotation></semantics></math> and odd <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2d-13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2d-13</annotation></semantics></math>, we only have Tate twists of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>, and ordinarity means <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\nmid\tau(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∤</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\nmid\tau(p)</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Ramanujan <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-function, i.e., <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(p)</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>th Fourier coefficient of the Ramanujan cusp form <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. It is not known whether there are infinitely many primes <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(p)\equiv 0\mod p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>mod</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(p)\equiv 0\mod p</annotation></semantics></math>.
The set of such primes less than <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>10</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{10}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{2,\,3,\,5,\,7,\,2411,\,7758337633\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 2411</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 7758337633</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{2,\,3,\,5,\,7,\,2411,\,7758337633\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In degree <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d-15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d-15</annotation></semantics></math>, we also see Tate twists of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math> (but only when <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 15</annotation></semantics></math>). In these cases, ordinarity means that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> also does not divide <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{p}(\Delta_{16})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{p}(\Delta_{16})</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>th Fourier coefficient of the weight <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> cusp form of level 1 for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}_{2}(\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}_{2}(\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. The set of non-ordinary primes for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math> that are less than <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{2,3,5,7,11,13,59,15271,187441\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>59</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15271</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>187441</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{2,3,5,7,11,13,59,15271,187441\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It is not known whether there are infinitely many non-ordinary primes for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We include semisimplicity as part of the generalized Tate conjecture, i.e., the action of <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}(L^{s}/L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}(L^{s}/L)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}((\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})_{L^{s}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}((\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})_{L^{s}},\mathbb{Q}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> is semisimple in each of the cases covered by Theorem <a href="#ThmTheorem3" title="Theorem C. ‣ 1.1. Main statements ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ On the Hodge and Tate conjectures for moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Summary table</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following table summarizes the degrees <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in which the generalized Hodge and Tate conjectures are known for <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all (or almost all) values of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as the motivic structures (Hodge structures and Galois representations) that appear, and the relevant references. The notation for the motivic structures is:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{L}:=H^{2}(\mathbb{P}^{1}),\quad\mathsf{S}_{12}:=W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{M}_{1,11}),\quad\mbox{ and }\quad\mathsf{S}_{16}:=W_{15}H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{1,15})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext> and </mtext><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{L}:=H^{2}(\mathbb{P}^{1}),\quad\mathsf{S}_{12}:=W_{11}H^{11}(\mathcal{M}_{1,11}),\quad\mbox{ and }\quad\mathsf{S}_{16}:=W_{15}H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{1,15}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Each is to be viewed as a rational vector space with an associated Hodge structure on the complexification and a continuous action of the absolute Galois group <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> on the tensor product with <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, for each prime <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. We fix <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d:=3g-3+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d:=3g-3+n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the generalized Tate conjecture, we assume that <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a finitely generated field and if <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> has characteristic <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\neq\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\neq\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=11</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d-j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d-j</annotation></semantics></math> for odd <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq 13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq 13</annotation></semantics></math>, the further restriction is that <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\nmid\tau(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∤</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\nmid\tau(p)</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d-15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d-15</annotation></semantics></math>, we also require that <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is ordinary for <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{16}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> does not divide <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{p}(\Delta_{16})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{p}(\Delta_{16})</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>th Fourier coefficient of the weight 16 cusp form of level 1 for <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SL}_{2}(\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SL}_{2}(\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
